  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Consider the standard matrices of three linear transformations shown below. Which of the transformations map ${R}^{2}$ onto a line?\n\n$$\n{T}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,{T}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{T}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 9 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "Given a linear transformation over ${R}^{2}$ with the standard matrix $T,$ we have the following:\n\n- $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto ${R}^{2}$ if and only if $\\text{rank}(T)=2,$ i.e., $T$ is nonsingular.\n\n- $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto a line if and only if $\\text{rank}(T)=1.$\n\n- $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto $(0,0)$ if and only if $\\text{rank}(T)=0,$ i.e., $T$ is the zero matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- ${T}_{1}$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto a line. Notice that the columns of ${T}_{1}$ are linearly dependent but it's not the zero matrix. So, $\\text{rank}({T}_{1})=1.$\n\n- ${T}_{2}$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto $(0,0)$ since ${T}_{2}$ is the zero matrix, i.e. $\\text{rank}({T}_{2})=0.$\n\n- ${T}_{3}$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto ${R}^{2}.$ Indeed, ${T}_{3}$ is a nonsingular matrix, i.e. $\\text{rank}({T}_{3})=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"${T}_{1}$ only.\"",
